course: dictionary
lesson: 11314
title: Yer: how people really talk
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Writers use yer to show how a character really speaks. «Get yer coat.» Yer is the spoken way of saying your, so it means put on your coat.
explain: «Are yer coming?» Here yer means you. Read the words around it and ask: would your or you fit better? The sentence is asking if you are coming.
explain: Yer is informal. It tells the reader about a character's accent or casual way of talking, so it belongs in stories, comics and speech. In your own school writing, use your and you.
fact: Yer is an informal, spoken spelling of «your» or «you».
fact: «Get yer coat» means «Get your coat».
fact: «Are yer coming?» means «Are you coming?»
fact: Yer can also stand for «you're» or «you are», so always check the sentence to see which meaning fits.
fact: Writers use yer to show accent and casual talk, especially in parts of the UK and Ireland.
exercise: «Get yer coat.» What does yer mean here? | Yours | *Your | You're | Yet
exercise: «Are yer coming?» What does yer mean here? | Yours | *You | Yet | Your
exercise: Why would a writer use yer in a story? | It is a long word | It means happy | *It shows the accent or casual way a character talks | It is always correct in school essays
test: Which formal word replaces yer in «Get yer coat»? | Yours | Yer | *Your | You're
test: Why is yer mainly used in stories and comics? | It is correct in school essays | It is a long word | It means happy | *It shows the accent or casual way a character talks
exam: «Are yer coming?» Which word means the same as yer here? | Yes | Yesterday | Your | *You
